var SaltCore=SaltCore||{};SaltCore["journey-deity-hermes"]=(()=>{var s=Object.defineProperty;var r=Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor;var h=Object.getOwnPropertyNames;var d=Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty;var l=(t,e)=>{for(var a in e)s(t,a,{get:e[a],enumerable:!0})},m=(t,e,a,n)=>{if(e&&typeof e=="object"||typeof e=="function")for(let o of h(e))!d.call(t,o)&&o!==a&&s(t,o,{get:()=>e[o],enumerable:!(n=r(e,o))||n.enumerable});return t};var c=t=>m(s({},"__esModule",{value:!0}),t);var u={};l(u,{journey:()=>i});var i={id:"deity-hermes",kind:"deity",paths:["hellenic","roman"],deityRef:"traditional/deity/hermes",title:"Hermes: Stones at the Crossing",summary:"A lunar month with Hermes: the trickster baby of the Homeric hymn, the god of herms and boundaries, messenger, luck-bringer and guide of souls, and how to honour him on the fourth day with a stone, a coin and the last libation of the night.",welcome:`This is a month of study and devotion with Hermes, the Greek god of roads and boundaries, messages and exchange, luck, wit and thieves, and the guide of souls to the underworld. He is the god who crosses: between people, between places, between the living and the dead.

It is a self-dedication, not an initiation. No priesthood stands behind it, and it does not replace any group\u2019s initiation. In Greek religion Hermes was everyone\u2019s god in a small way, honoured by travellers, traders, shepherds, athletes and the bereaved without anyone joining anything.

You will need a few small stones, a coin, a little olive oil or honey, a cup for a libation, and a sense of humour. He will test it.`,about:"Hermes was the son of Zeus and the nymph Maia, born in a cave on Mount Kyllene in Arcadia. On his first day he invented the lyre from a tortoise shell and stole his brother Apollo\u2019s cattle, then talked his way out of it. He became the messenger of the gods and the giver of good things. His oldest worship was probably at heaps of stones by the road, and his most famous image was the herm, a pillar with his head and a phallus, which stood at doors, crossroads, boundaries and gymnasia all over Greece. He was Psychopompos, guide of souls, who leads the dead to Hades and brought Persephone back; Agoraios of the marketplace; Enodios of the road; Diaktoros, the messenger. The fourth of every month was his. Rome knew him as Mercury. The later Hermes Trismegistus of the Hermetic writings grew from a fusion with the Egyptian Thoth, a related but distinct figure. Today Hellenic polytheists honour him with stones, coins, incense and libations, especially at the end of the day, and many others turn to him for travel, communication and luck.",notes:["Hermes is a god of boundaries and the dead as well as luck and trade. The journey gives his role as guide of souls the same care as his tricks.","Herms were phallic. That was ordinary in Greek religion and is described plainly here.","Hermes Trismegistus, the \u201Cthrice-great\u201D Hermes of the Hermetica, is a later Greco-Egyptian figure. He is related to Hermes but not the same, and his texts are not ancient Greek cult.","Hermes is a patron of thieves and tricksters in the myths. Devotion to him is not permission to cheat anyone. His honest side, fair exchange, is central.","Ancient curse tablets sometimes invoked Hermes as a binding power. This journey keeps to devotional and protective work."],holyDays:["noumenia","anthesteria","deipnon"],stages:[{kind:"threshold",title:"A stone on the heap",intro:"Greek travellers added a stone to the heaps by the road where Hermes was honoured. The journey begins the same way: a small heap of stones at your door or shrine, a libation and an introduction. If you can, begin on the fourth day of the lunar month, counting the Noumenia, the first crescent, as day one: his birthday.",days:2,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"hermes-threshold-stones",title:"Gather three stones",instructions:"On a walk, pick up three small stones from somewhere they are plentiful and you are allowed to take them, such as a garden, a beach or a gravel path. Not from a protected site, a grave or a trail cairn. Carry them home in your pocket.",times:1,prompt:"Where did your stones come from? What were you thinking about as you walked?"}],rite:{title:"Introduction at the heap",summary:"Purify, begin with Hestia, build a little heap of stones for Hermes, introduce yourself with a coin and a libation, and pour the last drops for Hestia.",minutes:15,ingredients:[{name:"A bowl of fresh water and a match for khernips",ref:"traditional/vessel/bowl"},{name:"A candle"},{name:"Three small stones"},{name:"A coin"},{name:"A cup of wine, milk or water with honey"},{name:"A dish for the libation",ref:"traditional/vessel/offering_bowl"},{name:"A little frankincense",ref:"traditional/herb/frankincense",optional:!0}],preparation:"Choose a place for his shrine near your front door or on a shelf in a hallway: Hermes guards the comings and goings. Wash your hands and set out the candle, cup, dish, stones and coin.",steps:[{title:"Make khernips",instructions:"Quench the match in the water, wash your hands and touch the water to your face.",spoken:"Clean hands at the crossing."},{title:"Begin with Hestia",instructions:"Light the candle and pour a drop for Hestia. The Homeric hymn asks Hestia and Hermes to dwell together in the house: she the centre, he the doorway.",spoken:"Hestia, first and last: I begin with you.",light:!0},{title:"Build the heap",instructions:"Place the three stones one on another, or side by side, at the shrine. As you lay each one, say a word.",spoken:"For the road. For the door. For the crossing."},{title:"Call Hermes",instructions:"Light the incense if you are using it. Raise your hands, palms up.",spoken:"Hermes, son of Maia, luck-bringing messenger of the gods, guide on the road, giver of good things: I am [your name]. I give you this coin and these stones, and I have come to learn who you are. Be my guide while I learn.",minutes:2},{title:"Give and pour",instructions:"Set the coin on the heap. Pour most of the libation into the dish. Sit for a few minutes.",minutes:4,timed:!0},{title:"Close with Hestia",instructions:"Pour the last drop for Hestia. Thank them both and put out the flame.",spoken:"Hestia, last as you were first. Hermes, thank you.",snuff:!0}],closing:"Pour the libation outside on earth. Leave the stones and coin on the shrine; you will add to them through the month. Write a few lines about how it felt to begin.",safety:"Never leave a candle or incense unattended, especially near a door with draughts and coats. Keep coins and small stones out of reach of small children and pets, who may swallow them."}},{kind:"study",title:"Learning the crosser",intro:"Ten days of reading: the baby god of the Homeric hymn, the herms and boundaries, Hermes the messenger and guide of souls, and his later Roman and Egyptian forms.",days:10,lessons:[{id:"hermes-hymn",title:"The baby who stole the cattle",summary:"The Homeric Hymn to Hermes: birth on the fourth, the tortoise lyre, the theft and the bargain with Apollo.",minutes:8,sections:[{heading:"Born on the fourth",body:`The long Homeric Hymn to Hermes, probably from the sixth century BCE, is one of the funniest poems in Greek. Maia, a shy nymph who lived in a shadowy cave, bore Zeus a son: \u201Cof many shifts, blandly cunning, a robber, a cattle driver, a bringer of dreams, a watcher by night, a thief at the gates.\u201D He was \u201Cborn with the dawning, at mid-day he played on the lyre, and in the evening he stole the cattle of far-shooting Apollo on the fourth day of the month.\u201D

That is why the fourth day of every month was his. The hymn gives you his whole character in one line: cunning, quick, at home at night and at the gates.`},{heading:"The tortoise and the lyre",body:`As the baby steps over the threshold of the cave, he meets a tortoise. He laughs, greets it as a lucky find, kills it, and makes its shell into the first lyre, with strings of sheep gut. \u201CIt was Hermes who first made the tortoise a singer,\u201D the hymn says.

Notice where this happens: on the threshold. Hermes\u2019 first act is at a doorway, turning a chance meeting into a gift. A lucky find was called a hermaion, a \u201CHermes thing.\u201D When you find a coin in the street, an old Greek would have thanked Hermes.`},{heading:"The theft and the sacrifice",body:`That night he drives off fifty of Apollo\u2019s cattle, making them walk backwards and wearing sandals of twigs to confuse the tracks. By a river he kills two of them and roasts the meat, then divides it into twelve portions, one for each of the gods, himself included. Though he longs for the smell of the meat, he does not eat it. Then he slips back into his cradle like a mist through a keyhole.

Scholars think this scene may explain a real sacrifice, perhaps at Olympia, and it shows Hermes as a god of fair shares and proper division, even while he steals. He is honest about the portions.`},{heading:"The bargain",body:`Apollo tracks him down, furious. The baby swears he knows nothing about cattle; he was born yesterday. Zeus laughs and orders them to make peace. Hermes plays the lyre, and Apollo, enchanted, trades the cattle for it. Apollo becomes god of the lyre; Hermes becomes lord of herds, receives a golden wand, and is made messenger to Hades.

So the hymn ends in exchange, not punishment. That is Hermes: trickery that ends in a fair trade and a friendship. A shorter hymn (18) closes with a line you can use in prayer: \u201CHail, Hermes, giver of grace, guide, and giver of good things!\u201D`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Homeric Hymn IV gives the birth on the fourth, the tortoise lyre, the cattle theft, the twelve portions and the exchange with Apollo. A Linear B tablet from Pylos names a god e-ma-a2, read as Hermes, so he was worshipped in the Bronze Age.",unknown:"Which real sacrifice the twelve portions reflect is debated. The hymn\u2019s date and place of composition are uncertain. Whether his name comes from herma, a heap of stones, is a strong theory but not proven."},inPractice:"Many devotees read the Homeric Hymn to Hermes aloud on the fourth of the month, and keep a tortoise image or a small lyre on his shrine. Thanking Hermes for a lucky find, a hermaion, is a simple daily habit.",reflect:["What do you make of a god who steals and then divides the meat fairly?","What was your last hermaion, your last lucky find?"],further:["Homeric Hymn IV to Hermes, trans. H. G. Evelyn-White (1914), free on Project Gutenberg","Norman O. Brown, Hermes the Thief (1947)","Lewis Hyde, Trickster Makes This World (1998)"],library:["traditional/deity/hermes","traditional/deity/apollo"]},{id:"hermes-herms",title:"Herms, heaps and boundaries",summary:"Stone heaps by the road, the herm pillars, the mutilation of 415 BCE, and Hermes at doors and borders.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"Heaps of stones",body:`The Greek word herma meant a stone or a heap of stones, and many scholars think Hermes\u2019 name is connected to it. By roads and at borders, travellers added a stone to heaps that marked the way and the boundary between territories. These heaps were sacred to Hermes, and food left at them for him might be eaten by hungry travellers, who called it a hermaion, a gift of Hermes.

So Hermes began, as far as we can tell, as the god of the marked way: the place where one person\u2019s land ends and the road begins, where strangers pass and meet.`},{heading:"The herm",body:`By the sixth century BCE the heap had become the herm: a square stone pillar topped with a bearded head of Hermes, with an erect phallus on the front. In Athens, the tyrant Hipparchos set up herms halfway between the city and each village, inscribed with short moral sayings, according to Plato\u2019s Hipparchus. Herms stood at front doors, crossroads, gateways, boundaries and gymnasia.

The phallus was not obscene to Greeks. It was a sign of luck, fertility and protection, turning away harm from the doorway. Many herms were anointed with oil, hung with garlands or given small offerings.`},{heading:"The night of the herms",body:`In 415 BCE, on the eve of Athens\u2019 great naval expedition to Sicily, most of the herms in the city were mutilated in one night. Thucydides says their faces were cut about, and Aristophanes jokes about their phalluses too. Thucydides describes the panic that followed. It was seen as an omen of disaster and a conspiracy against the city, and it helped drive the general Alcibiades into exile. The Sicilian expedition ended in catastrophe.

The scandal shows how much the herms mattered. Attacking them was attacking the safety of every door and road in Athens.`},{heading:"Hermes at your door",body:`Hermes Propylaios, \u201Cbefore the gate,\u201D guarded doors alongside Hekate and Apollo Agyieus. He was called Strophaios, \u201Cof the hinge,\u201D and Enodios, \u201Cof the road,\u201D and Agoraios, of the marketplace, and Hermes of the boundary stones.

You can bring this home simply. Keep a small stone pillar, a heap of stones or a little herm figure near your front door. Anoint it with a drop of oil on his day. Touch it as you leave for a journey. Add a stone when you come back safely.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Stone heaps and herms are well attested in literature and archaeology. Plato\u2019s Hipparchus (228\u2013229) describes Hipparchos\u2019 inscribed herms. Thucydides (6.27\u201329) describes the mutilation of 415 BCE. Epithets like Propylaios, Enodios and Agoraios are attested.",unknown:"The link between his name and herma is a theory. Who mutilated the herms in 415 was never settled. How the stone heap turned into the pillar is not clear."},inPractice:"Modern devotees often keep a small herm or stone heap at the front door and anoint it with olive oil on the fourth of the month. On walks they do not build or disturb cairns on hiking trails, which guide walkers and harm habitats; they add stones only to a heap at home or in their own garden.",reflect:["What boundaries do you cross every day? Which do you guard?","How do you feel about the herm\u2019s phallus? Why might it have meant protection?"],further:["Thucydides, History of the Peloponnesian War 6.27\u201329","Plato, Hipparchus 228\u2013229","Robin Osborne, \u201CThe Erection and Mutilation of the Hermai\u201D (1985)"],library:["traditional/deity/hermes","traditional/deity/hekate"],premade:["kitchen-threshold-salt-blessing"]},{id:"hermes-psychopomp",title:"Messenger and guide of souls",summary:"Hermes as messenger, guide of Priam and Odysseus, leader of the dead, and the last libation of the day.",minutes:8,sections:[{heading:"The messenger",body:`Hermes is Diaktoros, the messenger, with winged sandals, a traveller\u2019s hat and the kerykeion, the herald\u2019s staff, later shown with two snakes twined around it. In the Odyssey, Zeus sends him across the sea to tell the nymph Calypso to release Odysseus. He gives Odysseus the herb moly to protect him from Circe\u2019s magic. He slays the hundred-eyed giant Argos to free Io, which is why he is Argeiphontes, \u201Cslayer of Argos.\u201D

In the Iliad\u2019s last book he guides the old king Priam through the Greek camp at night, unseen, so that Priam can beg Achilles for the body of his son Hector. It is one of the most tender scenes in Homer: Hermes as the guide who gets the grieving safely across enemy ground.`},{heading:"Leader of the dead",body:`Hermes is Psychopompos, guide of souls. At the start of the last book of the Odyssey, he leads the souls of the dead suitors down to the underworld. In Samuel Butler\u2019s translation, which uses the Roman names, \u201CMercury of Cyllene summoned the ghosts of the suitors, and in his hand he held the fair golden wand with which he seals men\u2019s eyes in sleep or wakes them just as he pleases.\u201D The ghosts follow squeaking like bats as \u201CMercury the healer of sorrow led them down into the dark abode of death.\u201D

He also brings Persephone back up from Hades in the Homeric Hymn to Demeter. He is the one god who travels freely between the living and the dead. Grave reliefs often show him taking the dead by the hand.`},{heading:"Hermes Chthonios",body:`As Chthonios, \u201Cof the earth,\u201D Hermes received offerings for the dead. On the last day of the Anthesteria festival in Athens, called Chytroi, \u201Cpots,\u201D people cooked a pot of mixed grains and seeds and offered it to Hermes Chthonios on behalf of the dead, who were believed to walk the city during the festival, and then sent them away.

Some curse tablets of the classical period also called on Hermes Katochos, \u201Cthe restrainer,\u201D as an underworld power. That is part of the honest record. In this journey, Hermes Chthonios is honoured as the kind guide who takes the dead safely to rest.`},{heading:"The last libation of the night",body:`In the Odyssey, when Odysseus arrives at the palace of the Phaeacians, he finds the nobles \u201Cmaking their drink offerings to Mercury, which they always did the last thing before going away for the night.\u201D Hermes was the god of sleep and dreams too, with the wand that seals men\u2019s eyes.

This is a lovely custom to revive. Before bed, pour a few drops of water or wine for Hermes, asking for safe sleep and good dreams, and for safe passage through the night. It is the core of this journey\u2019s nine-night practice.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Odyssey 5, 7, 10 and 24, Iliad 24 and the Homeric Hymn to Demeter show Hermes as messenger, guide and psychopomp. The Chytroi offering to Hermes Chthonios is attested in later sources on the Anthesteria. Curse tablets invoking Hermes are known.",unknown:"How widely the last libation to Hermes was kept outside the Odyssey\u2019s Phaeacians is unknown. The details of the Chytroi and what was said are known only from late sources."},inPractice:"Many devotees honour Hermes Psychopompos when someone dies, lighting a candle and asking him to guide them safely. The bedtime libation is a common modern Hellenic practice built from the Odyssey. If a death is recent and the grief feels too heavy to carry, lean on people too: a friend, your doctor, or a bereavement service such as Cruse (UK) or a local grief line. Hermes guided Priam to someone who could help.",reflect:["Who would you want to guide you through hard ground, as Hermes guided Priam?","Is there someone who has died whom you would like to ask Hermes to guide?"],further:["Homer, Odyssey books 5, 7, 10, 24, trans. Samuel Butler (1900), free on Project Gutenberg","Homer, Iliad book 24","Sarah Iles Johnston, Restless Dead (1999)"],library:["traditional/deity/hermes","traditional/deity/persephone","traditional/deity/hades"],premade:["hedge-ancestor-candle-evening","hedge-letting-go-of-the-dead"]},{id:"hermes-later",title:"Mercury, Trismegistus and Hermes today",summary:"The Roman Mercury, the Greco-Egyptian Hermes Trismegistus, the caduceus confusion, and modern communities.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"Mercury",body:`The Romans identified Hermes with their Mercury, a god of trade whose name is related to merx, merchandise. His temple near the Circus Maximus was dedicated on 15 May, and at the Mercuralia merchants sprinkled their goods and their heads with water from his spring using a sprig of laurel, asking for profit and for forgiveness of any sharp dealing in the past year.

That last detail is honest and human: traders knew that their god of profit was also a god of tricks, and they asked to be forgiven. In Gaul and Britain, Mercury became one of the most popular gods of all, merged with local Celtic gods.`},{heading:"Hermes Trismegistus",body:`In Hellenistic and Roman Egypt, Greeks identified Hermes with Thoth, the Egyptian god of writing and wisdom. From this fusion came Hermes Trismegistus, \u201Cthrice-great Hermes,\u201D the legendary author of the Hermetica: philosophical and religious texts written in Greek in Egypt, mostly in the first to third centuries CE, along with many astrological and alchemical works.

Hermes Trismegistus is a real and important figure in the history of Western esotericism, and he deeply influenced the Renaissance and modern occultism. But he is not the Hermes of Greek cult. The phrase \u201Cas above, so below,\u201D often credited to him, comes from the Emerald Tablet, a text first known in Arabic in the early medieval period. If you are drawn to the Hermetic tradition, it is a path of its own.`},{heading:"The caduceus",body:"Hermes\u2019 herald\u2019s staff, the kerykeion or caduceus, with two snakes and often wings, is now used by many medical organisations, especially in the United States. That is a confusion with the rod of Asclepius, a staff with one snake, the true symbol of the healing god. Hermes\u2019 staff is a sign of heralds, messages, safe conduct and exchange. It is a small thing, but devotees enjoy getting it right."},{heading:"Who honours him now",body:`Hellenic polytheists honour Hermes on the fourth of the month with incense, honey, stones and coins, at the herm or stone heap at the door, at the Anthesteria for the dead, and with the bedtime libation. Roman polytheists keep the Mercuralia. Eclectic Pagans and witches often honour him on Wednesdays, the Roman day of Mercury (dies Mercurii), for communication, travel and study, and some treat him as a patron of magic through his link with Trismegistus.

All of these are real practices, and they draw on different strands. What they share is a god of movement, words and exchange who loves cleverness and fair dealing.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Ovid\u2019s Fasti (5.663\u2013692) describes the Mercuralia. The Hermetica were composed in Roman Egypt; the Emerald Tablet first appears in Arabic. The caduceus and the rod of Asclepius are distinct ancient symbols.",unknown:"How early the identification of Hermes and Thoth began is debated. Wednesday devotion to Hermes is Roman and modern, not Greek."},inPractice:"Set up his shrine near the door: a small herm or stone heap, a coin dish, a candle, a cup for libations, and perhaps a winged sandal or tortoise image. Add a stone or coin after each safe journey.",reflect:["Which strand of Hermes draws you: the Greek trickster, the Roman merchant god or the Hermetic sage?","Where do you need forgiveness for sharp dealing?"],further:["Ovid, Fasti 5.663\u2013692","Garth Fowden, The Egyptian Hermes (1986)","Brian Copenhaver (trans.), Hermetica (1992)"],library:["traditional/deity/hermes","traditional/deity/mercury","traditional/deity/thoth"],premade:["cosmic-wednesday-mercury-study","folk-thoth-offering"]}],practices:[{id:"hermes-study-hermaion",title:"Notice the hermaia",instructions:"Through this stage, each time something lucky or useful comes your way unexpectedly, say \u201Cthank you, Hermes\u201D and note it down.",times:10,every:"day",prompt:"What hermaia came today?"},{id:"hermes-study-shrine",title:"Set up his shrine at the door",instructions:"Set up a small shrine near your door with a stone heap or herm, a coin dish, a candle and a cup.",times:1,prompt:"What does it change to pass his shrine as you come and go?"}]},{kind:"practice",title:"Nine last libations",intro:"For nine nights, the Phaeacian custom: the last drink-offering of the day to Hermes. Then a lesson on his days, offerings and ethics.",days:12,lessons:[{id:"hermes-days-offerings",title:"His days, offerings and fair exchange",summary:"The fourth of the month, the Anthesteria and Hermaia, what to give, his titles, and the ethics of a trickster god.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"The fourth and the year",body:`The fourth day of each month, counted from the Noumenia, was Hermes\u2019 birthday. It was shared with Aphrodite, Eros and Herakles, so many devotees honour Hermes and Aphrodite together on it. A regular monthly offering at the herm or stone heap on the fourth is the backbone of his worship.

His yearly days include the Anthesteria in late winter, whose last day honours Hermes Chthonios and the dead; the Hermaia, a festival of the gymnasium and of boys\u2019 games held in many cities; and the Roman Mercuralia on 15 May. The app marks the Noumenia and the Anthesteria.`},{heading:"What to give",body:`Traditional offerings include incense, honey, milk, cakes, wine, olive oil to anoint a herm, and in sacrifice the animals\u2019 tongues, as befits the god of speech. In the Odyssey, the swineherd Eumaeus sets aside one of seven portions of the meal \u201Cfor Mercury the son of Maia and the nymphs.\u201D Coins and small stones are a good modern offering, recalling the stone heaps and the god of trade.

A fair and old principle: give him a share of windfalls. When something lucky comes your way, a find, a bonus, an unexpected gift, give a portion of it in his name, as a coin on his shrine or a gift to a traveller or someone in need. That keeps the exchange going.`},{heading:"His titles",body:"Good titles for prayer: Diaktoros, the messenger; Argeiphontes, slayer of Argos; Psychopompos, guide of souls; Chthonios, of the earth; Enodios, of the road; Agoraios, of the marketplace; Propylaios, before the gate; Kyllenios, of Kyllene; Eriounios, luck-bringer; Dolios, the crafty; Logios, of eloquence; Enagonios, of contests; Kriophoros, the ram-bearer, a shepherd\u2019s title. Call him Enodios before a journey, Logios before a difficult conversation, Agoraios for fair dealing, Psychopompos for the dying and dead."},{heading:"Honouring a trickster honestly",body:`Hermes is the patron of thieves and liars in the myths. That does not mean he blesses cheating. In the hymn, his theft ends in a fair exchange and a friendship, and he divides the meat honestly. Roman merchants asked Mercury to forgive their sharp practice. The lesson many devotees take is: be clever, be quick, be playful, and deal fairly.

He can also be honoured with humour. A joke offered at his shrine, a clever word game, a light touch in a tense moment: these are very Hermes. Expect him, too, to play with you now and then, with lost keys that turn up in odd places and timely coincidences.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"The fourth of the month, the Chytroi of the Anthesteria and the Mercuralia are attested. Offering tongues to Hermes is mentioned in ancient comedy and scholia. Eumaeus\u2019 portion is in Odyssey 14.",unknown:"The details of local Hermaia festivals vary and are known patchily. Giving a share of windfalls is a modern practice in keeping with the hermaion idea, not a recorded ancient rule."},inPractice:"Mark the next Noumenia and count four days to his monthly day. Choose a share-of-windfall rule for yourself, such as one coin in his dish for every lucky find.",reflect:["Which of his titles do you most need?","Where in your life could exchange be fairer?"],further:["Jon D. Mikalson, The Sacred and Civil Calendar of the Athenian Year (1975)","Homer, Odyssey 14"],library:["traditional/deity/hermes","traditional/herb/mint","traditional/crystal/citrine"],premade:["folk-noumenia-blessing","kitchen-solitary-evening-reflection"]}],practices:[{id:"hermes-nine-libations",title:"Nine last libations",instructions:"For nine nights, as the very last thing before bed, go to his shrine. Pour a few drops of water, wine or milk with honey into his dish, and say a short word: thanks for the day\u2019s comings and goings, and a request for safe passage through the night and good dreams. Touch the stone heap. In the morning, pour the dish out on earth outside.",times:9,every:"day",prompt:"What came and went today? What did you dream?"}]},{kind:"discernment",title:"At the boundary stone",intro:`Take a few days to stand at the boundary and look both ways.

Stopping here is a good outcome. So is honouring Hermes on the fourth without dedicating, or crossing to another god or path. Hermes is the god of free movement between places. He would be the last to insist you stay.`,days:3,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"hermes-discern-walk",title:"A walk to a boundary",instructions:"Walk to a boundary near you: the edge of your neighbourhood, a bridge, a crossroads, a park gate. Stand on it. Ask yourself the questions below. Walk home a different way.",times:1,premade:"hedge-crossroads-choice",prompt:"What did the boundary feel like? Which way did you want to go?"}],questions:["Am I drawn to Hermes himself, or mainly to luck and success? Have I met the guide of souls too?","Can I keep his fourth day and his bedtime libation for a year?","Do I enjoy his humour, or does it unsettle me?","Am I ready to deal fairly in his name, especially with money and words?","If I waited, would I feel relief or loss?"]},{kind:"dedication",title:"Dedication at the herm",intro:`If you have chosen to go on, make your dedication on the fourth day of a lunar month, at his shrine by the door. This is a self-dedication: your own promise. It is not an initiation and makes you no priest. Groups that initiate, including Hermetic orders, have their own rites, and this does not replace them.

It is fitting to do it in the evening, so that the dedication ends with the last libation of the night.`,days:1,lessons:[],practices:[],rite:{title:"Self-dedication to Hermes",summary:"Purify, begin with Hestia, add a stone to the heap, anoint it with oil, call Hermes by his titles, make your promise with a coin, and end with the last libation of the night.",minutes:35,ingredients:[{name:"A bowl of fresh water and a match for khernips",ref:"traditional/vessel/bowl"},{name:"A candle"},{name:"A new stone, from a journey this month"},{name:"A little olive oil for anointing"},{name:"A coin"},{name:"A honey cake or a little bread with honey",ref:"traditional/vessel/dish"},{name:"A cup of wine, milk or water with honey"},{name:"Frankincense",ref:"traditional/herb/frankincense",optional:!0},{name:"Your written promise"}],preparation:"During the month, keep a stone from a journey for this rite. Clean his shrine and the doorway. Write your promise: for example, to keep his fourth day, to give the last libation each night when you can, to deal fairly, to share your windfalls, to help travellers and to honour the dead. Wash and dress.",steps:[{title:"Purify",instructions:"Make khernips, wash your hands and face, and sprinkle a few drops at the threshold.",spoken:"Clean hands at the crossing."},{title:"Begin with Hestia",instructions:"Light the candle and pour a drop for Hestia.",spoken:"Hestia, first and last: I begin with you, keeper of the centre, as he keeps the door.",light:!0},{title:"Add the stone and anoint",instructions:"Add the new stone to the heap. Touch a drop of olive oil to the top stone.",spoken:"A stone from my road, for the god of roads."},{title:"Call Hermes",instructions:"Light the incense if using it. Raise your hands, palms up.",spoken:"Hermes, son of Maia, Diaktoros, Argeiphontes, Enodios of the road, Agoraios of fair dealing, Psychopompos, guide of souls, giver of grace and giver of good things: if ever I have poured you the last cup of the night, hear me. I come with a promise.",minutes:2},{title:"Make your promise",instructions:"Read your promise aloud. Set the coin on the heap and the cake in his dish.",spoken:"This I promise freely. Guide me on the road as I keep it."},{title:"Close with Hestia",instructions:"Pour a drop for Hestia and thank her. Put out the candle.",spoken:"Hestia, last as you were first.",snuff:!0},{title:"The last libation",instructions:"Just before you sleep, pour the last of the cup for Hermes, as the Phaeacians did, and ask for safe passage through the night.",spoken:"Hermes, the last cup of the night is yours. Bring me safely to morning."}],closing:"In the morning, pour the libation onto earth. You may share the honey cake, as Olympian offerings could be shared, or leave it outside. Keep the coin on the heap. Write down your promise and any dreams.",safety:"Never leave the candle or incense unattended, and keep them away from coats and draughts by the door. Keep coins and stones away from small children and pets."}},{kind:"ongoing",title:"On the road with him",intro:`Your devotion now lives on the road and in the month. Keep his fourth day at the herm or stone heap. Pour the last libation of the night when you can. Add a stone after safe journeys. Share your windfalls. Call on him as Psychopompos when someone dies, and keep the Chytroi of the Anthesteria for the dead.

Once a year, on the fourth nearest your dedication, renew your promise, change it or set it down with thanks. If you drift, pick up a stone on a walk and bring it home. He is always passing by.`,days:30,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"hermes-ongoing-fourth",title:"His monthly fourth",instructions:"On the fourth day of each lunar month, anoint the herm or stone heap with oil, give incense and honey, and add a coin.",times:12,every:"month",prompt:"Where did you travel, and what came your way this month?"},{id:"hermes-ongoing-anthesteria",title:"The pots of the dead",instructions:"On the last day of the Anthesteria, cook a small pot of mixed grains or beans and offer it to Hermes Chthonios on behalf of the dead. Do not eat it.",times:1,every:"holy-day"},{id:"hermes-ongoing-rededicate",title:"Yearly rededication",instructions:"Repeat the dedication rite on the fourth nearest your dedication date.",times:1,every:"month",prompt:"What roads has a year with Hermes taken you down?"}]}],sources:["Homeric Hymn IV to Hermes, trans. H. G. Evelyn-White (1914), Project Gutenberg #348: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/348 (quoted: \u201Cof many shifts, blandly cunning, a robber, a cattle driver, a bringer of dreams, a watcher by night, a thief at the gates\u201D; \u201Cborn with the dawning, at mid-day he played on the lyre, and in the evening he stole the cattle of far-shooting Apollo on the fourth day of the month\u201D; \u201CIt was Hermes who first made the tortoise a singer\u201D)","Homeric Hymn XVIII to Hermes, trans. H. G. Evelyn-White (1914), Project Gutenberg #348 (quoted: \u201CHail, Hermes, giver of grace, guide, and giver of good things!\u201D)","Homer, Odyssey, trans. Samuel Butler (1900), Project Gutenberg #1727: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1727 (quoted: book 7, \u201Cmaking their drink offerings to Mercury, which they always did the last thing before going away for the night\u201D; book 14, \u201Cfor Mercury the son of Maia and the nymphs\u201D; book 24, \u201CMercury of Cyllene summoned the ghosts of the suitors\u2026\u201D and \u201CMercury the healer of sorrow led them down into the dark abode of death\u201D)","Homer, Iliad 24; Homeric Hymn to Demeter","Thucydides 6.27\u201329; Aristophanes, Lysistrata 1093\u20131094; Plato, Hipparchus 228\u2013229; Ovid, Fasti 5.663\u2013692","Norman O. Brown, Hermes the Thief (1947)","Garth Fowden, The Egyptian Hermes (1986)","Sarah Iles Johnston, Restless Dead (1999)","Walter Burkert, Greek Religion (1985)"]};return c(u);})();
